 i want to have some  conversation at the end um  i'm going to transition into the third  part which is just how does this apply  to community  horticulture projects specifically in  the context of cornell garden based  learning working with our  cornell cooperative extension offices so  i'm just going to focus on briefly  two programs that are ones that's  already been developed and has a really  great track record  called seed to supper which is our  essentially our curriculum a beginner's  guide to  low-cost vegetable gardening but it's  really about partnership and community  building  and then a new program called the food  forest trial garden program which is a  little bit more on the  um i guess community food forest kind of  side that we talked about a little bit  earlier  so just to reiterate again see this  supper  is a really again complex community  based program that involves a lot of  steps  essentially we provide a curriculum  and a six course  six course uh series that we helped to  get  educators prepared for um statewide  but we really are providing again kind  of a community-based model for this so  it's very unique in every county that we  run this program  it first starts with getting a satellite  partner which is our cornell cooperative  extension offices  and they host essentially this program  often will apply for our mini grants  they then  work with us to advertise to community  participants and also getting garden  educators which are  our volunteers many of them happen to be  master gardeners  and we put them through kind of a garden  education preparation  every winter and sometimes  when we have a student course which we  do this year we engage cornell students  in actually preparing the educators  across the state  and then finally those same garden  educators  work with host agencies like  neighborhood associations  like current community gardening  associations  food pantries many different partners  that already work  in the food security arena to implement  the program so it's a great model for  again how we can kind of build these  partnerships  and there's an evaluation of of our ctis  for participants  before and after the course to see how  that's really helped to shape  their gardening experience and  you know their ability to grow food so  there's a wonderful kind of  again model already set up here in new  york state for this kind of work  um and there's there's a new program  that i'm working on developing with a  number of community partners and  statewide partners called the food  forest trial garden which i'm hoping to  build on the success of seed to supper  sites  really adding an element of climate  adaptation education  and food security education to that  program  so we're hoping through this program  again to develop a series of advanced  master gardener volunteer training  topics on ecological garden  and so this is a neat area we found  through a lot of surveying and a lot of  work  with with partners we have a community  horticulture work group through our  sustainable landscapes program work team  we're developing kind of this network of  experts on this particular topic  or across the state and we just had our  first meeting  and have been reviewing our cornell  garden based learning library course  which is our  master gardener training to see where  are the gaps what are some potential new  areas we could be educating people on  and and like i said we've done a number  of these surveys  with partners across the state and  looked at the feedback we've gotten  and here's some of the next steps that  came out of that  we have a couple of advanced topic  courses that we want to add again to  our core training based on the feedback  that we've gotten  water management again rain gardens and  xeriscaping  are a big area that master gardeners  want to learn more about  soil health and depth and climate change  and plant selection are huge  areas that got a lot of a lot of votes  from other participants in the course  as well as integrated pest management  specifically bio control  and then of course the design principles  behind how to really apply these skills  in a  specifically a food forest trial garden  which is a demonstration garden  and master gardeners across the state  understand this and  this is really a concept that many  cornell cooperative extension offices  even here at tompkins they have a great  demonstration garden area where you can  learn about it a lot of different  gardening techniques  so this is building on kind of advanced  whereas we have our seed to supper which  is beginning gardening  this is taking it to a next level with  more advanced training  for these master gardener volunteers and  as a result we're hoping to have a plant  selection list that can be implemented  in a second and third year so the final  piece is here  in year two and 2022 we'll host a  demonstration site installation again  getting that experiential learning for  our educators across the state  we'll provide in year three a mini grant  program that will take the  the resources developed and the plant  selection list  and share those with our educators  statewide in cornell cooperative  extension offices  so they can implement their own small  scale  demonstration site and my hope is also  that we can add to the research and  evaluation elements  here in the horticulture section like  the woody plants database we're hoping  that maybe there's some very successful  plants in these gardens that we could  add  to that database as well  so finally we're kind of wrapping up  here i would love just to leave some  extra time for folks to ask questions  but um the food forest trial garden  partners has kind of talked about at the  beginning  the partners helping out in this initial  first year phase of  assessing are the sustainable landscapes  pwt  and some new student hires with  landscape architecture and horticulture  background  we're working with key counties that  have experts in this area  um cce tompkins and schuyler and tioga  steuben  we're hoping to then plan and engage  them along with master gardener  volunteers  in an implementation workshop of a food  forest trial garden site  and like i said evaluate the education  and learning that happens as a result of  this  that can then feedback to on the  horticulture section  and uh these are just a few of the  really fantastic partners that we're  interviewing and talking to about future  workshops  but um we have some really wonderful  people working in the state  i get to work with all these wonderful  folks and i'm really hopeful that  we can get some input from all of you  all so i believe  i'm at time and i would love to  open up for our question and answer and  hear what you have to think about  um participatory action research is a  method for this kind of research in  community horticulture  all right i think i'll open it up  anybody ask me i actually um  thank you so much for this awesome talk  uh it  reflects really well i'm taking this  climate change and human development  class right now  in dsosh and um so i noticed that you're  speaking especially in all of these  community-based projects  it's within the critical discourse uh  surrounding climate change where  a lot of like government agencies i've  noticed in businesses especially  they operate more in the biophysical  discourse where it's more about  okay this is the problem that's  happening to our climate this is the  solution that we're going to  use and it more focuses on economic gain  so i was wondering um within that how  you see kind of moving forward with  these programs and how  because you have worked with government  organizations especially on the local  level  so how do you see transitioning that  into more community involvement  and and kind of bringing that more into  the mainstream in the future  yeah no that's a great question because  honestly um  working with local governments exactly  you're completely right i think  um what's really challenging about it is  it's mostly about the bottom line  um and i think for a lot of local  governments again they'll do a carbon  assessment  they'll really like they'll see where  their largest you know  potential um carbon  you know issues and and they'll really  focus in on that more on a technological  piece but  from a perspective of again resiliency  all that  the literature and the um the best  practices really look at  how do we engage people in in developing  some of these resilient resiliency skill  sets  and so i think that action research and  this kind of work again it has to be  integrated into planning so as you saw  in the examples  in savannah we had a sustainability  planning effort that preceded this  right so there was a large effort to  survey and engage community neighborhood  associations to find out what did they  find most important what do they won't  want to do  and that influenced and informed the  plan we had the right political backing  at that time to do that  and again they had all this prep work in  this asset-based community development  work that  all the neighborhood associations  apparently went through extensive  training on this method  to develop neighborhood associations  over 40 in the city it was a huge  effort and they have a ton of very um  again  a very tight-knit community-based  network of  organizations that work with  neighborhood groups  throughout the city so it's very unusual  it was a very unusual city  to work in in that way because i think  it had this groundwork  for that kind of um and they had they  actually had a ton of uh  positions at the time for neighborhood  um  basically neighborhood outreach folks so  i think that's one way that we can  better  do this is that in in bigger cities  again really having  dedicated people like i the role i had  um  where i would be going out and going to  neighborhood meetings and  engaging directly with neighborhood  leaders  and we need that kind of hands-on  building of trust  in order to i think again translate  what's happening more at a  at a city government level with trying  to reduce carbon impact  to how does this really relate back to  my life like how does this relate back  to how it will impact me and i think  that's where the public health folks  are really helping out a lot with that  with a lot of these mapping tools and  kind of  practices at educating community members  about the impacts of climate change on a  very local level  and the way i was able to work with some  pretty pretty  um i would say regressive sorts of  policies  around things like green infrastructure  was to really just talk about flooding  we don't even talk about climate change  we just talked about flooding  and we talked about how it impacted  people's lives in a very like real way  and that bought in that really got a lot  of community buy-in  which then added to that kind of  political pressure  plus getting money doesn't hurt if you  get money from grants to do this kind of  work  it really helps i hope that answers a  little bit of your  of you guys thank you so much  um  is there anybody else who has any other  questions because i know there's a lot  of great experts and i tried to  again put a lot of information in but um  wanted to have more of a discussion  again about how this relates to  to your own work or against it for  students their interests  um if this is something that folks want  to learn more about and again partnering  with other disciplines like urban  planning like landscape architecture i  think there's a lot of  potential to really be  integrating both the plant sciences and  the social sciences so thank you  i have a question for you ashley can you  hear me  yeah um i in in trying to do and sort of  involve  people in participatory action research  have you found  or um have you found that um  it takes a while sometimes for  volunteers to understand the principles  of doing this and i'm sorry if i missed  this  discussion oh no no no no and and  honestly i didn't really  i think what one of the challenging  things about doing a seminar like this  is because  it's you know normally when we do uh a  workshop  in seed to supper um you know we really  first ask questions about the audience  and kind of get a sense of where people  are coming from  and so um that would be like that's  really again  where the volunteers um and again in  many of the communities  where i was working in savannah there  was not necessarily like a huge  environmental bent  um but really more of again um a social  justice bent to a lot of the  neighborhood organizations there  their leanings so  i i really found that to get people on  board with this i didn't talk about  participatory action research i really  just talked about  um you know here's some key areas that  we're hoping to look at  citywide to influence health and  sustainability  and we talked about like kind of these  five key areas and one of them happened  to be  more green infrastructure though we  might have used a less jargony term  um at the time and so really describing  to people  why this would be beneficial people get  community gardens pretty  pretty quickly because it's like we'll  have food and it's like  this is something people want they want  more community spaces and they want more  you know accessible food healthy food um  so that  those were not as hard of a uh i think  the buy-in wasn't as hard as again kind  of describing why we needed  um more trees in the city and why  they're so beneficial  and that there's different cultural  feelings about trees  in different areas related to safety  related to  all kinds of things that you might not  normally think of  so i think once people got and again i  have some really  really great kind of article i can share  that talks about some of the  the impacts on some of the local  community members who then got trained  in the full  kind of arborist training program um and  i think  they really show how the benefits of  this community  project speaks to the community again  when it was  all about job training and helping  people to start their own businesses  um there was an incredible amount of  buy-in from these communities that were  you know looking for looking for jobs  looking for opportunities so this is  something that there really wasn't a lot  more of in  in that part of the state or really um  there really were no organizations doing  that kind of work already unlike here i  think there's just  there's just um we're not like  we're not green infrastructure projects  going on so it was new  and it was providing economic  opportunity and so that was i think the  biggest  most positive way that we were able to  also then generate  some um from a volunteer perspective  also getting a lot of volunteers engaged  in from the communities to to reach out  and get people involved in the training  program that was  that was huge any other  any other questions  well i appreciate everybody um coming  out today and if you have any other  questions feel free i can put my um  email in the chat and feel free to reach  out for more information  this has been a production of cornell  university  on the web at cornell.edu


